  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The threat of strong to severe thunderstorms and flash\nflooding will continue through this evening. \n\nWidespread showers and thunderstorms continue to impact much of\nNew Jersey and portions of eastern Pennsylvania and Delaware \nthis afternoon. The greatest threat from these storms will\ncontinue to be localized flash flooding, with many reports of \nroad closures due to high water already. Some storms may also be\nstrong to severe with large hail and damaging winds. A Severe \nThunderstorm Watch remains in effect until 8 PM.\n\nThe latest hires guidance suggests that heaviest precipitation \nwill be generally along the I-95 corridor and points northwest. \nHires guidance has also pointed to areas along the I-78 corridor\npotentially seeing heavy rainfall amounts as well. Generally \naround 1-2 inches of rain with localized QPF values in excess of\n3 inches. Widespread flash flooding still seems unlikely, but \nthe localized threat remains elevated. The Flood Watch remains \nin effect for most of NJ and the I-95 corridor.\n\nFor severe hazards, latest CAMS and HREF guidance show SBCAPE\nvalues remaining around 1500-2500 J/kg with ensemble maximums \nin excess 3000 J/kg. Deep layer shear forecasts remain around \n30kt with a strongly veering profile aloft. Boundary layer flow\nis not particularly strong but surface winds are more backed \nthan previous forecasts so an isolated tornado is possible \nprimarily along and west of I-95. That said, damaging wind gusts \nand hail will remain the greatest threat. Dominant mode should \nbe multicell convection but a supercell can't be ruled out \nespecially where cloud bearing shear is maximized. SPC has \nexpanded the area of a slight risk (2 out of 5) to now include \nmuch of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front is expected to pass through the\narea on Friday.\n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards. There are differences between models in \nboth the timing, and if this will be a single cold front, or an \ninitial cold front with a reinforcing shot of cold air advection\ncoming in behind it. Saturday and Sunday appear to be dry across\nmost of the area with the exception of southern areas Saturday\nwhere low probabilities of showers and storms will exist.",
